<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Daily hydration</p>
    <h2>Build the habit around access, not a magic number</h2>
    <p>Water is continuously lost through breathing, urine, stool and sweat, and those losses need to be replaced. Total water also comes from food, including fruit, vegetables, soups and other beverages, so hydration is broader than the number of bottles a person drinks.</p>
    <p>Health Canada notes that most people consume enough total water to meet hydration needs, while physical activity and hot climates can increase requirements. That makes a rigid universal daily volume less useful than a routine built around thirst, meals, environment and activity.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/7592977/pexels-photo-7592977.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Young adult drinking water while sitting in a modern kitchen" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Make it easy</p>
    <h2>Small environmental cues beat complicated hydration rules</h2>
    <p>Keeping water visible, carrying a reusable bottle, drinking with meals and having fluid available before activity are simple ways to make hydration easier to maintain.</p>
    <p>Taste also matters. Some people drink more consistently when water has flavour. A prepared hydration mix can serve that preference, but flavour should not be confused with a medical need for extra electrolytes.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
