  <h2>Four common reasons fluid needs may rise</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Hot weather</h3><p>Higher temperatures and sweating can increase water loss, particularly during outdoor activity or physical work.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Long or hard activity</h3><p>Exercise can increase sweat losses enough that fluid and electrolyte planning becomes more relevant.</p><a href="/pages/active-living">Active Living →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Travel and disrupted routines</h3><p>Limited access, dry cabin air, long days and unfamiliar schedules can make regular drinking less convenient.</p><a href="/pages/travel">Travel →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Illness or medical conditions</h3><p>Fluid needs can change substantially, and some conditions require restriction rather than more fluid. Use clinical guidance when appropriate.</p><a href="/pages/clinical-hydration">Clinical Hydration →</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
